{{Quote|Forgive the programming. He's a little rough around the edges. But he is the best.|Ranzar Malk, on Q9-0|<ref name="Chapter 6" />}}
'''Q9-0''', also known as '''Zero''' or '''Z''', was a modified [[RA-90]] [[protocol droid]] who worked as a [[mercenary]] during the [[New Republic Era|time]] of the [[New Republic]]. Zero was part of a [[Ranzar Malk's crew|crew]] of mercenaries led by [[Ranzar Malk]], consisting of [[Migs Mayfeld]], [[Burg]], [[Xi'an]], and the [[Mandalorian]] [[bounty hunter]] [[Din Djarin]], who were sent by Malk to rescue [[Qin]], a [[prisoner]] aboard the [[New Republic Correctional Transport|New Republic prison ship]] ''[[Bothan-5]]''.

During the mission, the team betrayed Djarin and left him behind, and Zero, who stayed aboard the bounty hunter's [[gunship]], ''[[Razor Crest]]'', discovered [[Din Grogu|Grogu]], an [[Youngling|infant]] who Djarin had previously [[Incident on Nevarro|rescued]] from a [[The Client|client]] of the [[Bounty Hunters' Guild]]. Zero attempted to [[Death|kill]] the infant, but was destroyed by Djarin, who had managed to escape.

The wreckage of Zero remained in storage aboard the ''Razor Crest'' for some [[time]], and a [[Frog Lady|passenger]] who Djarin was tasked with transporting later used the droid's [[vocabulator]] to communicate with the bounty hunter while stranded on the [[planet]] [[Maldo Kreis]].

==Biography==
===A rescue mission===
{{Quote|It's not possible. Even for the ''Crest''.''"<br />"''That's why he's flyin'.|Din Djarin and Ranzar Malk, on Q9-0|<ref name="Chapter 6" />}}
A former [[protocol droid]], Q9-0 turned to a life of crime,<ref name="Handbook">''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian Handbook]]''</ref> working as a [[mercenary]] during the [[New Republic Era|time]] of the [[New Republic]].<ref name="Chapter 6" /> In the [[Standard year|year]] [[9 ABY]],<ref name="Date" /> Zero was part of a [[Ranzar Malk's crew|team]] of mercenaries recruited by [[Ranzar Malk]] to [[Bothan-Five incident|rescue]] [[Qin]], an associate who had been [[Prison|imprisoned]] aboard<ref name="Chapter 6" /> the [[New Republic Correctional Transport]] ''[[Bothan-5]]''.<ref name="Chapter 10">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 10: The Passenger}}</ref> The mission was to be led by the former [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] [[Sniper|sharpshooter]]<ref name="Chapter 6" /> [[Migs Mayfeld]],<ref name="Chapter 14">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 14: The Tragedy}}</ref> and the crew consisted of the [[Devaronian]] muscle [[Burg]] and the [[Twi'lek]] [[Xi'an]]. Malk also hired the [[Mandalorian]] [[bounty hunter]]<ref name="Chapter 6" /> [[Din Djarin]],<ref name="Chapter 8">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 8: Redemption}}</ref> requiring the use of his [[gunship]], the ''[[Razor Crest]]'', as it was not on the grid of either the Galactic Empire or the New Republic.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

[[File:Zero_Razor_Crest.png|thumb|left|320px|Zero pilots the ''Razor Crest''.]]
The mercenaries gathered aboard<ref name="Chapter 6" /> the [[Roost]]<ref name="Roost">{{Databank|ranzar-ran-malk|Ranzar "Ran" Malk}}</ref> [[space station]], where Malk operated, and Mayfeld introduced Djarin to Zero. Zero inspected the ''Razor Crest'', finding a distorted [[Hologram|holographic]] message from [[Greef Karga]] of the [[Bounty Hunters' Guild]] in its communications system. While the team planned the mission, Zero disembarked from the ''Razor Crest'', remarking on several issues with the [[Starship|ship]]. Zero, who was chosen to [[pilot]] the ''Razor Crest'' due to the maneuvers required to land on the prison [[transport]] without being detected, reassured Djarin of his superior response time. Once the crew had boarded the gunship, Zero piloted the ship out of the station's [[hangar]] and into [[hyperspace]].<ref name="Chapter 6" />

Some [[time]] later, Zero dropped the ''Razor Crest'' out of hyperspace<ref name="Chapter 6" /> in the vicinity of the [[Dilestri system]],<ref name="Chapter 14" /> approaching ''Bothan-Five''. After [[Cloaking device|cloaking]] the ship's signal, Zero maneuvered the ''Razor Crest'' along their planned path, though the sudden movements of his piloting unsettled his passengers. After the ''Razor Crest'' landed on the prison transport, the team boarded the vessel while Zero waited aboard the gunship, accessing the transport's systems. Zero directed the team to the control room, disabling surveillance on board the prison ship. When the crew reached the control room, Zero was reluctant to open the door, detecting an organic signature inside. The crew entered regardless, finding the New Republic [[New Republic soldier|soldier]] [[Lant Davan]], who was [[Death|killed]] by Xi'an after a brief standoff. As he died, Davan activated a [[Homing beacon|tracker]], and Zero informed the crew of a New Republic [[distress signal]] homing in on them.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

===Betrayal===
{{Quote|Zero to Mayfeld. Mayfeld, do you copy? It seems comms are no longer functioning, therefore you cannot hear me. You are on your own.|Q9-0, shortly before noticing the Child|<ref name="Chapter 6" />}}
The crew found Qin's cell, rescuing the prisoner. However, the mercenaries turned on Djarin, trapping him in the cell and leaving him for the incoming New Republic attack team. Meanwhile, Zero continued to look at the ''Razor Crest''{{'s}} communications, viewing Karga's full message, which spoke of making a delivery to a [[The Client|client]]. Djarin managed to escape the cell, and Zero warned Mayfeld of the situation. The ''Razor Crest''{{'s}} communications ceased to function, preventing the crew and Zero from making contact. Behind him, Zero noticed the [[Youngling|infant]]<ref name="Chapter 6" /> [[Din Grogu|Grogu]],<ref name="Chapter 14" /> the subject of Karga's message, who Djarin had [[Incident on Nevarro|rescued]] shortly after delivering him to the Client.<ref name="Chapter 3">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 3: The Sin}}</ref> Interpreting the [[bounty]] on Grogu that the message spoke of to be a priority, Zero resolved to kill the infant.<ref name="Character Encyclopedia U&E" />

[[File:Zero_destroyed.png|thumb|right|300px|Zero is destroyed by Din Djarin.]]
Zero picked up his [[blaster rifle]] and left to search for Grogu, who was hiding elsewhere in the ship. While Zero hunted for the infant, Djarin managed to defeat the other members of the team, locking them in a cell aboard ''Bothan-Five''. Zero located Grogu in a hidden compartment of the ship, targeting him with his rifle. However, Djarin boarded the ''Razor Crest'' and shot Zero in the back, destroying the protocol droid before he could fire.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

===Post-destruction===
{{Quote|Do not be alarmed. I bypassed the droid's security protocols and accessed its vocabulator.''"<br />"''What the hell are you doing? That droid is a killer.|"Frog Lady," speaking through Q9-0, and Din Djarin|<ref name="Chapter 10" />}}
[[File:FrogLadyQ90-TMCh10.png|thumb|left|300px|Frog Lady reprogrammed Q9-0 in order to access his vocabulator.]]
Djarin kept Zero's broken body aboard the ''Razor Crest'', stored in a pile within the ship's hold. After the ''Razor Crest'' was damaged and became stuck within an [[Water|ice]] [[cave]] on the [[planet]] [[Maldo Kreis]] while Djarin was transporting the being known as "[[Frog Lady]]" and her [[Egg|spawn]] to the [[moon]] of [[Trask]], Frog Lady attempted to communicate the urgency of her journey, but the Mandalorian was unable to understand her [[Frog (language)|language]]. As Djarin slept in the hold of the wrecked ''Razor Crest'', Frog Lady bypassed Zero's security protocols in order to use the droid's [[vocabulator]] to communicate.<ref name="Chapter 10" />

The bounty hunter awoke to the sound of Zero's voice and aimed his [[blaster]] at the wreckage, believing the droid himself to be speaking. Using Zero's vocabulator, Frog Lady reassured Djarin, explaining that her family line would become extinct if her eggs were not taken to safety. While Djarin was shocked over her usage of the droid due to the danger that Zero had posed, Frog Lady managed to convince him to repair the damaged ''Razor Crest''. They eventually escaped the cave, though the hold of the vessel was left open to [[Realspace|space]] during their journey to Trask.<ref name="Chapter 10" /> The ''Razor Crest'' fell into the [[ocean]] upon landing on Trask<ref name="Chapter 11">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 11: The Heiress}}</ref> and was eventually repaired,<ref name="Chapter 12">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 12: The Siege}}</ref> but the vessel and most of Djarin's belongings aboard it were [[Battle of Tython (9 ABY)|destroyed]] by [[turbolaser]] fire from the [[Gideon's light cruiser|light cruiser]] of [[Moff]] [[Gideon]] on [[Tython]].<ref name="Chapter 14" />

==Characteristics==
{{Quote|My response time is quicker than organics. And I'm smarter, too.|Q9-0|<ref name="Chapter 6" />}}
[[File:Q90-SWI215.jpg|thumb|right|200px|Zero felt that he was superior to organics.]]
Q9-0, nicknamed Zero and Z by his colleagues,<ref name="Chapter 6" /> was a protocol droid with modified programming, operating with precision timing.<ref name="Databank" /> As a droid, Q9-0 had a faster response time and a higher intellect than organic beings, a fact Zero was keen to point out. He was able to use this to his advantage when piloting, and could make maneuvers that were impossible for others to do. Zero was able to operate a starship by connecting to it via a socket, and did not need to physically operate the controls. During the rescue of Qin, Zero [[Slicer|sliced]] into the transport's systems, and was able to deactivate security measures on the ship.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

Zero's programming was described as "rough" by Ranzar Malk. The droid boasted about his superiority over organics to Din Djarin, and Malk reassured Djarin that Zero was the best despite his programming. The droid also openly insulted the state of the bounty hunter's ship, the ''Razor Crest'', pointing out its faults and questioning why the use of the ship was required. Q9-0 stood at 1.8 [[meter]]s,<ref name="Chapter 6" /> or 5 [[Foot|feet]] 11 inches,<ref name="Character Encyclopedia U&E" /> and had a bipedal form, with two [[Limb|arms]] and two legs. His plating was dark gray in [[color]],<ref name="Chapter 6" /> and he had two large [[Insect|bug]]-like [[photoreceptor]]s behind silver-colored mirrored domes. He also possessed a recharge port in the center of his torso, as well as a communications antenna atop his head.<ref name="Character Encyclopedia U&E" /> Zero spoke with a voice of calm authority.<ref name="Junior Novel">[[The Mandalorian Junior Novel|''The Mandalorian'' Junior Novel]]</ref> The droid also had security protocols built into his systems.<ref name="Chapter 10" />

==Equipment==
{{Quote|We have much better ships. Why are we using this one?|Q9-0, on the ''Razor Crest''|<ref name="Chapter 6" />}}
Zero carried an [[EE-3 carbine rifle]], and wore several equipment pouches strapped to his torso,<ref name="Chapter 6" /> several of which contained blaster repairs. He also had a [[vambrace]] as part of the plating on his wrist.<ref name="Character Encyclopedia U&E" /> Ranzar Malk owned a number of starships at his space station, which Q9-0 was familiar with piloting. While carrying out the rescue of Qin, Zero flew the ''Razor Crest'', the gunship owned by Djarin.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

==Behind the scenes==
[[File:MalkCrewConceptArt.jpg|thumb|right|290px|Concept art featuring Q9-0 by Brian Matyas]]
Q9-0 first appeared in [[Chapter 6: The Prisoner|Chapter 6]] of the [[2019]] television series ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]'',<ref name="Chapter 6" /> which aired on [[December 13]], 2019.<ref name="DTCI">{{WebCite|url=https://lumiere-a.akamaihd.net/v1/documents/the_mandalorian_project_one-sheet_10_ec1e005f.pdf|text=The Mandalorian Media Kit|date=2019-09-24|format=[[Wikipedia:PDF|PDF]]|work=[[Wikipedia:Walt Disney Direct-to-Consumer & International|Walt Disney Direct-to-Consumer & International]]|pages=[https://lumiere-a.akamaihd.net/v1/documents/the_mandalorian_project_one-sheet_10_ec1e005f.pdf#page=1 p. 1]|archivedate=20210623202717}}</ref> The character was voiced by [[Richard Ayoade]] and performed by [[Chris Bartlett|Christopher Bartlett]]. While only identified as Zero in the episode itself, the name of Q9-0 was given in the credits.<ref name="Chapter 6" /> Bartlett claimed that, out of his various appearances in the series, Q9-0 was his favorite character to portray.<ref name="Bartlett-Twitter">{{Twitter|chrisfbartlett|status/1243202413403582465|[[Chris Bartlett|Christopher Bartlett]]|quote=For my Star Wars friends in the UK waking up to bingeing The Mandalorian, look for my characters! My favorite is #Q9Zero|archivefile=File:Christopher-Bartlett-Mandalorian-March-26-2020.png}}</ref> Concept art of Ranzar Malk's crew, including Q9-0, was created by [[Brian Matyas]].<ref name="Concept Art">{{SW|url=series/the-mandalorian/chapter-6-concept-art-gallery|text=The Mandalorian: Chapter 6 Concept Art Gallery}} {{C|Slide 5}}</ref>

A droid also portrayed by Bartlett<ref name="Bartlett-Twitter" /> with a similar appearance to Q9-0 previously appeared in the [[Chapter 3: The Sin|third]] chapter of the series,<ref name="Chapter 3" /> released on [[November 22]], 2019,<ref name="DTCI" /> though no connection was made between the two droids. The droid participates in the Bounty Hunters' Guild's attempt to prevent Djarin escaping with Grogu,<ref name="Chapter 3" /> which Q9-0 is unaware of in Chapter 6.<ref name="Chapter 6" />

